{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261025T190407Z-e86edd5b", "ts": "2026-10-25T19:11:41Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.7.3-dev.1", "branch": "feature/1052-evidence-transport"}, "body": {"base_commit": "8fbdfbc6d0d6cb3ad177008b020c54b4856db98b", "base_tree": "0f9260e6c405ad8533e59b81f89ac055496a70a8", "head_tree": "7bab0b305d33551c4cc7c647079955c0ab47d3c6", "head_commit": "d1ef94ca518d2070b5441563861abcf0d6f18f13", "dispatch_commit": "d1ef94ca518d2070b5441563861abcf0d6f18f13", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "tests/test_binary_skew_guard.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync_dispatch.py", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py", "tests/test_hot_path_git_batching.py", "tests/test_plan_check_claims.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_import_status.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_pr_reviewer.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_session_start_ingest.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-status.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", "documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "tests/test_binary_skew_guard.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync_dispatch.py", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py", "tests/test_hot_path_git_batching.py", "tests/test_plan_check_claims.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_import_status.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_pr_reviewer.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_session_start_ingest.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-status.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "Ingest makes any well-formed line on a fetched notes ref verdict authority; the inbound trust boundary is recorded nowhere", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the wave recorded the egress leg of the transport (security-model.md, egress_boundary) but not the inbound one. `evidence_notes.ingest` (run at every boundary session start by `_session_start_ingest`, and by `evidence ingest`/`evidence sync`) appends every line on `refs/notes/prawduct` or any `refs/notes/<remote>/prawduct` that passes `_is_envelope`. That check covers kind/id/int schema/dict body only, and those lines become facts that `check-cumulative-critic` and the Stop gate trust. Nothing checks where a line came from. A hand-written `git notes --ref prawduct append` followed by a push (by a collaborator, or by an agent in any clone) mints a review fact that covers a tree in every clone that ingests: a teammate's machine, CI, a fresh checkout. The plan's governed_by disposition for data-model's 'no model sits in a fact's write path' says 'conforms: ingest appends code-written lines verbatim', which is a provenance claim the code never checks. security-model.md's Direction norm 'Untrusted governance state ... fetched references ... is data, not instructions' is not among governed_by at all. I am not rating this as exploitable (BLOCKING): the people who can push the product remote's notes ref already hold its code, and in the writing clone the store file has no write guard either. What is missing is the decision. Remedy (instance): record the inbound trust boundary as a vetoable decision in security-model.md beside the egress line. Name who can mint facts for every ingesting clone (anyone with push to the product remote's `refs/notes/prawduct`) and why that is accepted. Then restate the data-model disposition as the shape check it actually is. If the owner rejects that trust, the construction is a provenance check at ingest, not a longer disposition.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "sent_envelope's scratch-tree test is a looser copy of the predicate it feeds; a durable fact from `<x>/worktrees/<name>` turns ephemeral in the receiving clone", "recommendation": "Why it broke: d1ef94ca spelled the ephemeral-parent test again (`\"worktrees\" in path.parts`) instead of using the one owner, `gitstate._under_claude_worktrees`, which needs `.claude` immediately before `worktrees`. Take a writer path `/home/u/worktrees/wf_login`, or `/home/u/worktrees/agent-<hex>` on a detached or scratch branch. It is not ephemeral in the writing clone, because it is not under `.claude/worktrees`. The note rewrites it to `.claude/worktrees/<name>`, so `evidence.is_ephemeral_fact` classifies it ephemeral in every receiving clone. That clone then reads the interval as uncovered while the writer reads it as covered, which contradicts the docstring's 'classifies it the same way in every clone'. It fails toward understating, and it needs harness-shaped directory names outside `.claude`, so it is rare. Remedy (instance): call `gitstate._under_claude_worktrees(path)` (or a public wrapper) in `sent_envelope`. Add the negative case beside `test_a_scratch_worktree_fact_keeps_its_ephemeral_tail_and_no_user`: a non-`.claude` `worktrees/wf_xxxx` path reduces to its bare name and `is_ephemeral_fact` agrees with the writer.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Chunk 06's follow-up backlog items are not recorded as filed anywhere in the bundle", "recommendation": "Chunk 06's deliverables name two backlog items: `critic-consolidate --github-status` with the owner ruling it needs, and Q1, a `verify-resolutions` anchor that does not depend on `.critic-findings.json`. Chunk 05's Decisions entry adds a third: `stats`/`contribute` double-counting ingested reviews at the collector. The plan's Decisions block and the commit messages record the live verification but no backlog ids for any of the three, and the local backlog cache (last written 12:24) shows none. I could not tell from the bundle whether they were filed, so this is a NOTE and not a dropped requirement. Remedy (instance): confirm all three exist and cite their ids in the Chunk 06 Decisions entry. If any is unfiled, file it now (core.md: 'File a backlog item the moment you decide it should exist'). The sustainability role's backlog reconciliation is the deeper check.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Migration shim for a refspec no released version ever wrote: configure's legacy-exact-refspec replacement", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the R-1 fix treated an exact refspec written by this branch's own unreleased Chunk 02 code as if it were a deployed configuration to migrate. Goal 7 names this case: \"Unnecessary backwards compatibility \u2014 migration paths or shims with no deployment to migrate \u2192 WARNING\". `configure` first appears in this bundle (ade88718), so `git branch -a --contains ade88718` lists only feature/1052-evidence-transport. prawduct-live has no evidence_sync.py, and this clone's remote.origin.fetch holds only the branch refspec. The cost is real. There is `_legacy_refspec`, plus the `replaces` key it adds to configure's `--json` contract (frozen once released, under additive-first). There is `_ere_literal`'s --unset-all path, the 'replaced \u2026 with' output branch, and four tests in tests/test_evidence_sync.py (lines 437-471 and 579-590). Doctor check #25 also tells every consumer's doctor to look for 'no exact line `+refs/notes/prawduct:refs/notes/<r>/prawduct` (an earlier version wrote it \u2026)', a line no consumer can have. Delete all of it, plus the matching assertion in tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py::test_configured_means_the_refspec_configure_writes_and_not_the_legacy_one. The same 'an earlier version wrote' clause is in api-contract.md's evidence-transport entry (oracle, for the builder's sweep). If a dev clone of the owner's still holds the exact line, remove it by hand with `git config --unset-all`. That is cheaper than shipping a migration nobody else needs.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Doctor #25 and onboard restate `evidence configure`'s own decision in prose, with three extra git grants to support it", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the skills were written to detect 'configured' themselves rather than ask the command that owns the predicate. Doctor #25 has the model do four things: pick the remote ('the only remote, else origin, else the first listed', a prose copy of gitstate.push_remote), read `git config --get-all remote.<r>.fetch` and `git config --get notes.rewriteRef`, judge the result against a restated refspec, and only then run the `evidence configure` preview. That preview already writes nothing and answers every branch of the check: no remote is 'ok' with its reason, 'nothing to change \u2014 already configured', or the list of changes. The duplication has already cost a round, since d1ef94ca/a483ab23 had to re-edit doctor in step with the refspec change. A test (test_evidence_transport_skills.py) now pins only the refspec half, and the remote-selection half is unpinned. If push_remote ever honours remote.pushDefault, doctor will read one remote's config while configure writes another's. Doctor could then call a clone 'configured' and never offer the fix. Replace the detection with 'run `prawduct-hook evidence configure`; nothing to change means configured; otherwise relay its lines and on the owner's yes run it with --apply'. Drop the `Bash(git remote)`, `Bash(git config --get-all remote.*)` and `Bash(git config --get notes.rewriteRef)` grants and the tests that pin them. Onboard's 'the target has a remote' precondition is the same restatement, because configure with no remote is already ok and does nothing. Keep only onboard's real precondition, that the session runs in the target. To find other members, search the skills for a `git config`/`git remote` read that comes before a `prawduct-hook` command which makes the same decision.", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "`evidence` ops are routed by two dispatchers with two usage-exit conventions", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the delegation partition (hook binary to B, evidence_sync to A) became the module structure. The hook's cmd_evidence routes `sync` and `configure` to evidence_sync, with an ImportError guard that fails closed. Everything else falls through to evidence.evidence_cmd, which routes `ingest` back up into evidence_notes through a lazy cyclic import, with no such guard. evidence.py's `_CLI_USAGE` then documents two ops it admits it never dispatches. The visible consequence is in the exit codes, which api-contract.md calls the contract. `evidence ingest --bogus` exits 1, the same code as a real ingest failure, while `evidence sync --bogus` and `configure --bogus` exit 2. A caller cannot tell a usage error from a failure for one op of the family, and once this ships, changing ingest's 1 to 2 repurposes an exit code. Before release, give the command one router: either all three transport ops in the hook beside sync and configure, or all in a small transport dispatcher in evidence_notes. Give `ingest` usage errors exit 2 like its siblings, and update the api-contract sentence in the same commit.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The notes-ref roster has three entry points: one is dead, and another is private but called from evidence.py", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the zero-subprocess on-disk reader was added for `evidence status`, but the fallback to git stayed in the caller rather than in the module that owns the roster. evidence_notes now has three answers to 'which notes refs does this clone hold': `_notes_refs` (one for-each-ref), `notes_refs_on_disk` (parses loose refs and packed-refs, returning None for reftable), and `ref_present`. `ref_present` has no caller in plugin/ or tools/, only test_evidence_notes.py:1031-1041. Core learnings rule: 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT'. Meanwhile evidence._print_note_transport rebuilds the on-disk-then-git fallback itself, calls the private `evidence_notes._notes_refs` across the module boundary, and gets the common dir from the store's layout (`path.parent.parent`). Give evidence_notes one public `notes_refs(project_dir, common_dir=None)` that owns the on-disk read and the git fallback. Have `_print_note_transport` call it, and delete `ref_present` and its test. Moving `_print_note_transport` into evidence_notes as the status renderer would also remove evidence.py's second lazy import of a module that imports it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "sent_envelope's scratch-path test is a second, looser spelling of gitstate's `.claude/worktrees` predicate", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: d1ef94ca checked `\"worktrees\" in path.parts` instead of calling the predicate whose owner says 'One body for every caller \u2026 so the directory half can never be spelled two ways' (gitstate._dir_label / _under_claude_worktrees). The sender keeps the tail for any path with a `worktrees` component, for example /home/u/worktrees/agent-abc123. It rewrites that to `.claude/worktrees/agent-abc123`, which the receiving clone's ephemeral_kind_of can classify as ephemeral even though the writing clone did not. The effect is display only: the '[ephemeral \u2014 covers no branch]' tag and status's wasted-cost line, since is_ephemeral_fact feeds no gate. That is why this is a note. Have sent_envelope keep the tail when gitstate's own `.claude/worktrees` test holds, made public or passed through ephemeral_worktree_kind_of_path's parent check, and drop `_SCRATCH_TAIL`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "evidence_notes' module docstring still says a note carries actor.worktree as its last path component", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: d1ef94ca added the `.claude/worktrees/<name>` exception in sent_envelope's body and its docstring's second paragraph, but not in the module docstring (line 18, 'travels as its last path component') or sent_envelope's opening sentence (line 89). This is prose that nothing reads, so it is a note, not a fix commit. Make the claim relational, e.g. 'reduced to a locator that names no user (sent_envelope)', rather than restating the rule. The same unqualified sentence is in api-contract.md's evidence-transport entry ('`actor.worktree` cut to its last path component'), which is an oracle record and the builder's to sweep.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A failed FIRST notes push leaves no durable signal, and the shipped consumer note says evidence status will show it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the O-17 fix compares the local ref against the push remote's tracking ref, and that tracking ref exists only after a fetch that found notes or a push that succeeded. So the most likely failure has no comparison to make: the first `evidence sync` against a remote with no notes yet, which is every product's first `/prawduct:pr create`. Trace it: the fetch matches nothing and no tracking ref is created; reconcile attaches locally; the push is refused, so `update-ref` never runs. `evidence status` then sees `refs/notes/prawduct` alone. `unpushed()` returns None, because `not any(r != NOTES_REF for r in refs)`, so no line is printed. `unattached` prints '0 transported fact(s) with no note on their commit'. The clone reads as healthy while nothing has left it, and every other clone and CI reads the branch as uncovered. `test_no_tracking_ref_prints_no_unpushed_line` pins this silence and justifies it with 'the no-note line already names the facts'. That holds for a clone that never attached anything, but not for one that attached and failed to push. The shipped fragment's consumer note (`.prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md`) promises the opposite: 'a failure there is reported without blocking the PR \u2014 `evidence status` then shows `N note commit(s) not yet pushed` until a sync succeeds'. That is false in exactly this case, and it ships as release-note text (Records Pass, 'it ships'). `api-contract.md` states the both-refs caveat correctly. hook-surface rule: 'A status surface that reports the ABSENCE of expected output must say whether absence is the normal in-flight state'. Fix: when the local ref exists and the push remote's tracking ref does not, print a line from local state alone, e.g. `rev-list --count refs/notes/prawduct` as 'N note commit(s) never pushed from this clone \u2014 run `prawduct-hook evidence sync`'. That is one local call, no network, and it is the normal in-flight state before the first PR, which the line should say. Then rename and invert the pinning test and make the consumer note match.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The plan records the keystone's R-2\u2026R-16 as 'closed by non-recurrence', a closure no mechanism records; nine findings stay undispositioned", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Records Pass ('misleads into action'). The Decisions block's last bullet says the keystone review rev-20261025T173802Z-7a0e5c49's 'R-2\u2026R-16 were confirmed not to recur and close by non-recurrence (no resolution record \u2014 the cumulative spans them)'. Chunk 02's entry promised these would be 'recorded by the verify pass at Chunk 06'. Neither happened: the verify was widened to a `final`, which writes no resolution facts. No `disposition` fact exists for R-2, R-3, R-6, R-11 (warnings) or R-7, R-8, R-9, R-10, R-16 (notes), checked against the evidence store. Nothing in `plugin/` implements closure by non-recurrence: `dispositions.py` counts a finding with no fact as `undispositioned`. So `render-dispositions --scope evidence-transport`, which `building.md` says goes into the entry, will report nine undispositioned findings, and the plan tells the next reader that nothing is owed. gates rule: 'A documented \"clears when X\"/\"exempt when X\" arm is NOT evidence X is implemented \u2014 grep the module first'. Fix: record the nine as facts. `--accept` with the fixing commit and 'confirmed absent by rev-20261025T182810Z-3266301c' works, since `--fixed` is refused on judgeable paths. Then make the bullet state what was recorded, not a mechanism that does not exist.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (evidence-transport, Chunks 01-06, through d1ef94ca)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. For the owner: the plan's mid-build decisions, what settled each, and the alignment-pass misses. (1) Chunk 04: `review_anchor` gets one home, now `evidence.review_anchor`. The merged check is two `for-each-ref` calls, so a merge seen only on `origin/<base>` counts. Three holes in the Goals' lifecycle rule (pending fragment, fork-point anchor, base-tip branch) were settled by LC1 and 'authority fails closed'. Alignment miss: the rule as written in Goals was incomplete. (2) Chunk 01: `evidence ingest` is refused from a scratch worktree, which withdraws the plan's 'runs anywhere' (alignment miss, the keystone's R-15). The bound includes the merge base. Schema-ahead lines are ingested whatever their kind. The note is fed on stdin. (3) Chunk 02: reconcile and the unsent count live in `evidence_notes`. Git runs with `LC_ALL=C` and `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`. The stuck-merge check is ours, because git does not refuse a new merge over an unfinished one (probed). `config --add` preserves foreign values. Imports are attributed by `actor.branch`. (4) Chunk 05: six budget ceilings were raised, each with a reason. A second builder-drafted [DECISION] covers the notes ref and the `.git/config` keys. Chunk 02's acceptance named a Critic check that does not exist and was struck (alignment miss). (5) Chunk 03: the R-6 TYPE_CHECKING guard is kept, because the plan's premise that the dead import alone justified it was wrong (alignment miss). The `check-claims` semantics change is consumer-visible and stated in the fragment. Session start on a foreign binary writes the store under the harness-invoked exemption. (6) Chunk 06 and after: the planned verify widened into a third full review (`final`). The nonfunctional-requirements disposition still reads 'one keystone ..., one boundary cumulative'. R-1's exact refspec became a pattern (probed, git 2.54). The O-16 privacy reduction is in: the worktree basename travels, the session does not. d1ef94ca keeps a `.claude/worktrees/<name>` tail, recorded only in its commit message. Settled by: the owner's delegation approval (2026-10-24), git probes, LC1, the fail-closed norm, and two builder-drafted [DECISION]s still open for veto (O-15, accepted on the record).", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "sent_envelope re-spells the scratch-worktree path rule ('worktrees' anywhere in the path) instead of asking gitstate's one decision function", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Learnings Cross-Check, hook-surface rule 'Copying a fix into a sibling procedure or reader is a NEW change \u2014 they share a regex, not invariants'. Also architecture's 'every fact has one home', which the plan's `governed_by` disposes as conforms. `gitstate.ephemeral_kind_of` is documented as 'THE decision function', with one body 'so the directory half can never be spelled two ways'. Its path test is `_under_claude_worktrees`, which requires the adjacent `.claude/worktrees` pair. d1ef94ca's `sent_envelope` instead tests `_SCRATCH_TAIL in path.parts`, i.e. any component named `worktrees`. A durable worktree at `~/worktrees/agent-x`, or one with a scratch-named branch, is sent as `.claude/worktrees/agent-x`. Every receiving clone then labels its reviews '[ephemeral \u2014 covers no branch]' in `evidence status`/`list` (display only; coverage does not read `is_ephemeral_fact`). The new test covers the `.claude` positive and a plain path, but has no case with `worktrees` outside `.claude`. Fix: call gitstate's predicate, making `_under_claude_worktrees` public or adding a thin public wrapper, and add that negative case.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Session-start ingest failure is a NOTE on stderr, which the harness does not inject, while the NOTE addresses the incoming agent", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why: session-start NOTEs default to stderr. This one names the briefing's reader as its audience ('the briefing reads this clone's store without the other clones' notes'), but a SessionStart hook that exits 0 has only stdout added to context. `cmd_clear`'s reflection notice makes that choice the other way, with the comment 'Continuity fact, so stdout: the agent starting now is the audience'. A persistent ingest failure (an unreadable listing, an unreadable store) is therefore invisible at every session start. It surfaces only when `/prawduct:pr`'s `evidence sync` fails at `step: ingest`. Until then the agent sees other clones' reviews as missing and may re-run reviews that already exist, which costs wall clock, the P0. Genuinely ambiguous, because its sibling advice NOTEs use stderr deliberately and `tests/test_session_start_ingest.py` pins stderr. To close the class, decide the channel from the reader each NOTE names, not per call site. Bound: grep `print(\"NOTE:` with `file=sys.stderr` inside `cmd_clear`'s boundary region.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "The 'every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees' class recurred in the commit after O-20 was dispositioned fixed; its construction was not built", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Learnings Cross-Check, core rule 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'. The rule's tell, 'your second fix of one class in two rounds', fits: R-15, then O-20, now again. O-20 asked for a construction: `Retires:` phrases in the fragment so `retired-claim-survives` catches survivors, and a test reading api-contract's step list against `evidence_sync`'s literals. Only its three instances were fixed. The fragment declares no `Retires:`, and no test reads api-contract. d1ef94ca then changed the note line again (a scratch wo